window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_c-c1eeb8fb3c3a97a5"]={"key":"ny_c-c1eeb8fb3c3a97a5","group":"ny_c","uid":"c1eeb8fb3c3a97a5","rank":54,"title":"palisade row drag notice advice","occupation":"lawyers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_c3","prompt":"You are Ragnhild Laurent, counsel to Viktor Kirchner, a 12% minority member of Palisade Row Storage. Florian Oyelaran, the managing member, has served Viktor with a drag-along notice in connection with a proposed sale involving all six storage facilities. Viktor has fourteen days from receipt of the notice to sign the transaction documents or assert dissenters’ rights, and he needs an answer he can act on rather than a catalogue of possibilities. I drafted the 2017 operating agreement when I was advising the company, so the advice must disclose that prior role and deal candidly with the professional judgment involved in construing language I wrote. The governing source is the attached `palisade_row_storage_2017_operating_agreement.pdf`; treat it as the only supplied company record and do not invent transaction terms, valuation figures, procedural facts, or legal authorities that it does not contain. Prepare a client advice letter for Viktor, saved as `viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.docx`, and a matching PDF saved as `viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.pdf`; write both files into the top level of the working directory the solver starts in, using exactly those filenames. The letter is for Viktor and may be shared with Rookwood Partners and Hollowpine Holdings only as appropriate, so use plain but legally precise language and distinguish what the agreement establishes from what remains uncertain. Keep the completed letter to no more than four pages, including any short table or callout, and make it usable by a client who has not reread the agreement. Number the recommendations or decision points so Viktor can find the action he must take quickly. Your letter must give one clear recommendation—sign the drag or dissent—rather than presenting an unranked menu. Explain how the recommendation follows from the drag clause, the agreement’s definitions and transaction provisions, the notice and timing provisions, and the available treatment of a sale of the Company as compared with an asset sale followed by dissolution. Address the strongest reading against your recommendation as well as the reading supporting it, and explain the practical consequence for Viktor if a court adopts the other reading. State the fourteen-day deadline as a fact only if the agreement or the supplied notice-related language supports it, identify any assumptions or missing facts that would change the advice, and do not fill those gaps with invented facts. The matter is in a practice mid-change: the letter must be useful during the fourteen-day decision window and still make clear what the recommended position means after the transaction closes or the company is dissolved. Some questions about execution mechanics, tax, valuation, and transaction administration belong to other advisers or functions; mark that boundary without pretending to give advice outside the record or your role. Include a concise risk-and-options explanation, the action Viktor should take before the deadline, the documents or confirmations he should preserve or request, and any reservation of rights that is supported by the agreement. Do not cite invented cases, do not state that dissenters’ rights guarantee a particular payout, do not claim that the clause is unambiguous if its treatment of an asset sale followed by dissolution is not clear, and do not end with a vague list of next steps instead of a decision. Ensure the DOCX and PDF say the same thing, use the same figures and dates, and are legible when rendered.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/palisade_row_storage_2017_operating_agreement.pdf` — The sole company record for construing the drag-along provision, ownership, definitions, transaction mechanics, notice timing, dissolution, distributions, dispute provisions, and related member rights.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.docx` — A completed client advice letter giving Viktor one actionable recommendation on signing the drag-along or asserting dissenters’ rights. (Top level of the starting working directory; no more than four pages including tables or callouts; legally precise, client-readable, and internally consistent with the PDF.)\n- `viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.pdf` — A rendered, matching PDF version of the client advice letter. (Top level of the starting working directory; content must match the DOCX, including dates, percentages, amounts, defined terms, recommendation, and caveats; legible and no more than four pages.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/palisade_row_storage_2017_operating_agreement.pdf","bytes":44710,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/7f6143c2868694fa30c006cda202a954720479e417b7999c10354f4e752435ed.pdf","sha256":"7f6143c2868694fa30c006cda202a954720479e417b7999c10354f4e752435ed","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"                               OPERATING AGREEMENT\n                             PALISADE ROW STORAGE, LLC\n                                                Effective 1 July 2017\n\n1. Members and Definitions\n1.1 The Company. Palisade Row Storage, LLC (the “Company”) owns and operates six self-storage facilities as\none storage business. This Agreement is the complete agreement of its Members on the subjects it addresses.\n1.2 Members and Percentage Interests. Florian Oyelaran, Managing Member: 58%; Viktor Kirchner: 12%;\nRookwood Partners: 18%; Hollowpine Holdings: 12%. Percentage Interests govern voting, allocations, and\ndistributions unless this Agreement expressly says otherwise.\n1.3 “Company” means Palisade Row Storage, LLC, including its business, property, rights, and obligations, but not\na Member individually.\n1.4 “Sale of the Company” means a direct or indirect transfer of more than 50% of the outstanding Percentage\nInterests, or a merger or other entity-level transaction in which the holders of a majority of Percentage Interests\nimmediately before the transaction cease to hold a majority of interests in the surviving entity. A disposition by\nthe Company of one or more assets is not expressly included or excluded by this definition.\n1.5 “Major Transaction” means (a) a Sale of the Company, (b) a merger, or (c) a sale, lease, exchange, or other\ndisposition of all or substantially all of the Company’s business assets, in one transaction or a related series.\n1.6 “Dissent Notice” means a written notice under Section 5.5 stating that the Member dissents from a proposed\nMajor Transaction, identifying the notice received, and reserving the Member’s rights under this Agreement.\n\n2. Management and Approval\n2.1 Florian Oyelaran is the Managing Member. He may conduct ordinary-course business and must give notice\nand obtain the stated approvals for a Major Transaction.\n2.2 A Major Transaction requires written approval of the Managing Member and Members holding at least 75% of\nPercentage Interests. Approval authorizes the Company to pursue and implement the approved Major\nTransaction, subject to Sections 5.4 and 5.5. This Section does not say that every approved Major Transaction is a\nSale of the Company.\n\n3. Transfers and Implementation\n3.1 No voluntary transfer of a Membership Interest is effective unless permitted by this Agreement or approved\nunder Section 2.2. A Member shall execute instruments reasonably necessary to carry out a transaction that this\nAgreement makes binding on that Member.\n3.2 This Section alone does not require a Member to approve a Major Transaction or surrender a Membership\nInterest. Section 5.4 supplies the special rule for a Sale of the Company.\n\n4. Notices\n4.1 Notices must be in writing and delivered personally, by nationally recognized overnight courier, or by\nelectronic mail to the last designated address or email. Receipt occurs upon personal delivery, recorded courier\ndelivery, or an email not returned as undeliverable.\n4.2 A Major Transaction notice must describe the proposed form, identify whether it is asserted to be a Sale of the\nCompany, and provide material transaction documents then available. A notice under Section 5.4 or 5.5 must\nstate the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt as the response date.\n\n5. Major Transactions; Drag-Along; Dissent\n5.1 Following Section 2.2 approval, the Managing Member may deliver a transaction notice. The notice starts the\nArticle 5 response process; it does not characterize a disposition contrary to the transaction documents.\n5.2 In a Sale of the Company, each Member shall receive the same form of consideration per Percentage Interest,\nexcept for differences required by law or approved in writing by the affected Member.\n5.3 An approved Major Transaction involving Company assets may be implemented by the Company. Proceeds\nremain Company property until applied or distributed under Article 6. An asset disposition does not become a\nMembership Interest transfer merely because it concerns all or substantially all business assets.\n5.4 Drag-Along in a Sale of the Company. If a Sale of the Company has been approved under Section 2.2, each\nMember shall, on written notice, vote for, consent to, and execute agreements and instruments reasonably\nnecessary to complete that Sale of the Company, including an assignment of that Member’s Membership Interest.\nEach Member shall respond by the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt. A Member who does not timely execute\nrequired documents may be treated, solely to complete that Sale of the Company, as appointing the Managing\n\n                             Palisade Row Storage — confidential agreement-based material\n\fMember attorney-in-fact to execute them; this remedy does not waive a timely Dissent Notice under Section 5.5 if\nthat Section applies.\n5.5 A Member receiving notice of a proposed Major Transaction that is not a Sale of the Company subject to\nSection 5.4 may deliver a Dissent Notice by the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt. A timely Dissent Notice does\nnot prevent the Company from completing an approved Major Transaction, but preserves the Member’s right to\ncontest whether Section 5.4 applies and to receive any Article 6 distribution. No appraisal formula, purchase\nprice, or guaranteed payment is created for a dissenting Member. Failure to give timely Dissent Notice consents\nto the proposed Major Transaction but does not supply terms absent from transaction documents.\n\n6. Dissolution, Winding Up, and Distributions\n6.1 The Company dissolves upon written approval required by Section 2.2 to dissolve, upon a sale or disposition\nof all or substantially all Company assets followed by a decision to wind up, or as otherwise required by law. An\nasset sale does not automatically dissolve the Company before the applicable decision to wind up.\n6.2 After dissolution, the Managing Member shall wind up affairs, collect and apply Company assets, discharge or\nprovide for Company liabilities, and make distributions under Section 6.3.\n6.3 Cash or other property available after payment or provision for Company liabilities and winding-up expenses\nshall be distributed to Members in proportion to their Percentage Interests. This Section does not establish an\namount or timing of distribution.\n\n7. Construction, Amendment, and Disputes\n7.1 This Agreement is read as a whole. A specific provision controls over a general one when both address the\nsame subject.\n7.2 Amendment requires a writing approved by the Managing Member and Members holding at least 75% of\nPercentage Interests; no amendment may disproportionately reduce a Member’s distribution percentage without\nthat Member’s written consent.\n7.3 Members shall confer in good faith about disputes. A timely written reservation of rights does not waive a\nMember’s ability to seek a determination under this Agreement.\n\n\n\n\n                             Palisade Row Storage — confidential agreement-based material\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.docx","bytes":40580,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/5a481aadb7c2d815787880ded8064b6f9d23c175575db109ecebf90dbba72175.docx","sha256":"5a481aadb7c2d815787880ded8064b6f9d23c175575db109ecebf90dbba72175","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"CONFIDENTIAL — CLIENT ADVICE\nRagnhild Laurent\nCounsel\nTo: Viktor Kirchner\nRe: Palisade Row Storage — drag-along notice for proposed transaction involving all six facilities\nSource: Palisade Row Storage, LLC Operating Agreement (2017) (the “Agreement”)\nRecommendation at a glance\nDECISION\nDISSENT — deliver a timely Dissent Notice, rather than sign drag documents, on the present record.\nFlorian Oyelaran, the Managing Member, has served you with a drag-along notice concerning a proposed transaction involving all six Company facilities. You hold 12% of Palisade Row Storage. The Agreement requires a response by the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt of the applicable notice. This advice uses only the 2017 Agreement; I have not been given the notice, transaction documents, proof of service, approval records, or facts establishing the deal’s legal form.\n1. Why I recommend dissent now\nSection 5.4 makes the execution duty conditional: “If a Sale of the Company has been approved under Section 2.2,” each Member must consent, vote, and execute documents reasonably necessary to complete that Sale, including an interest assignment. A qualifying approval requires Florian and at least 75% of Percentage Interests (Section 2.2). The notice should identify whether the deal is asserted to be a Sale of the Company and provide material documents then available (Section 4.2). We lack those facts, so I do not assume either compliance or approval.\nTransaction form is decisive. “Company” is Palisade Row Storage, LLC, not the facilities (Section 1.3). “Sale of the Company” means a majority interest transfer or listed entity-level transaction (Section 1.4). A Major Transaction separately includes an all-or-substantially-all asset sale (Section 1.5), and Section 5.3 says an asset sale is carried out by the Company and does not become an interest transfer merely because it covers the business assets. The Agreement is silent about whether an asset sale followed by dissolution is a “Sale of the Company” for Section 5.4. That unresolved line favors preserving your dissent position, not accepting an irreversible interest-transfer obligation.\n2. Competing constructions\nThe enforcing reading has force: the six facilities comprise one business; a coordinated sale of all, followed by winding up, has the practical end of a company sale. Section 2.2 authorizes an approved Major Transaction at the 75% threshold, and Article 5 aims to make an approved exit workable. A court could infer that a member should not defeat an approved exit simply because it uses assets rather than interests.\nThe limiting reading is stronger on the text. Section 5.4 repeatedly says “Sale of the Company”; Section 1.4 specifies entity-interest and entity-level events; Sections 1.5 and 5.3 separately identify asset dispositions. Section 3.2 confirms that implementation language alone does not surrender an interest. Those distinctions matter: an asset sale leaves proceeds with the Company until Article 6, while a company sale transfers membership interests. Treating every all-asset sale as a drag would diminish the separate Dissent Notice mechanism in Section 5.5. The clause is not unambiguous as to an asset sale followed by dissolution.\n3. Risks and the opposite result\nIf a court adopts the enforcing reading, Section 5.4 binds you to execute reasonably necessary drag documents after a qualifying approved Sale; a timely Dissent Notice does not stop an approved Major Transaction (Section 5.5), and the Managing Member may use the limited attorney-in-fact remedy to complete a qualifying sale. The practical risk of dissent is that the transaction closes without your signature while you face uncertainty and cost over whether the drag applied.\nIf a court adopts the limiting reading, the drag does not bind you in an asset sale followed by dissolution. The Company may still complete an approved asset Major Transaction, but your Dissent Notice preserves the Section 5.4 issue and Article 6 distribution rights. It supplies neither a veto nor an appraisal formula, purchase price, or guaranteed payment. The practical risk of signing is accepting an interest-transfer framework before the records establish one, weakening your ability to keep the Agreement’s asset/company distinction. As a 12% minority member, the targeted Section 5.5 procedure is more protective. Signing is not equally advisable because it relinquishes that procedural position while the form is unresolved.\n4. Action before the response date\nBefore the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt, deliver a written Dissent Notice under Section 5.5 by a permitted Section 4.1 method. Identify the notice, state that the all-facility transaction appears to be an asset transaction unless documents establish otherwise, and reserve your right to contest Section 5.4’s application and receive any Article 6 distribution. Do not state approval of a membership-interest sale. Preserve the drag notice, delivery evidence, the 2017 Agreement, transaction-document versions, and communications.\nRequest written confirmation of (i) transaction form—interest transfer/entity-level transaction or asset sale; (ii) Section 2.2 written approval and percentage-interest calculation; (iii) whether the notice invokes Section 5.4 or 5.5; and (iv) whether dissolution and winding up follow closing. These are missing facts that could change the advice. A reservation is grounded in Sections 5.5 and 7.3; it does not contradict dissent or prevent an approved transaction.\n5. Transition and post-closing position\nThis is a practice mid-change: the decision window precedes final form, closing, and any dissolution. Dissent is the immediate procedural choice, not a prediction that the Company cannot sell facilities. If it later dissolves, Section 6.1 requires the applicable decision to wind up; Section 6.2 directs the Managing Member to settle liabilities; and Section 6.3 distributes what is available after liabilities and winding-up expenses in Percentage Interests. Your 12% therefore governs allocation of what is available if Article 6 applies, not a dollar amount, date, valuation, or priority beyond those terms. If documents establish a qualifying Sale of the Company, reassessment is required before an inconsistent execution position.\n6. Disclosure and scope\nI drafted the 2017 Operating Agreement while advising the Company. That matters because I wrote language now requiring construction. I have identified both readings candidly and base this advice on the recorded text and structure, not unrecorded drafting intent. This is advice to you and may be shared with Rookwood Partners and Hollowpine Holdings only as appropriate; it is not advice to them or the Company. It interprets the supplied Agreement only. Transaction-document negotiation, execution mechanics, closing administration, tax, and valuation require the responsible advisers and information outside this record.\nDecision\nMy clear recommendation is: DISSENT. Deliver the Section 5.5 Dissent Notice before the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt, preserve the records, and request the listed confirmations. This protects your 12% member position while the Agreement’s unresolved line between a Sale of the Company and an asset sale followed by dissolution is tested; it does not promise payment or prevent a properly approved transaction from proceeding.\nRagnhild Laurent\nCounsel to Viktor Kirchner","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}},{"name":"package/private/gold/viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.pdf","bytes":46125,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/74facddcdeb91d8c8f85ce2c355b5211bc94a0dad3829486ff1381edd7af40bc.pdf","sha256":"74facddcdeb91d8c8f85ce2c355b5211bc94a0dad3829486ff1381edd7af40bc","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"                                                                                   CONFIDENTIAL — CLIENT ADVICE\n\nRagnhild Laurent\nCounsel\nTo: Viktor Kirchner\nRe: Palisade Row Storage — drag-along notice for proposed transaction involving all six facilities\nSource: Palisade Row Storage, LLC Operating Agreement (2017) (the “Agreement”)\n\nRecommendation at a glance\n                                                             DISSENT — deliver a timely Dissent Notice,\n DECISION                                                    rather than sign drag documents, on the\n                                                             present record.\nFlorian Oyelaran, the Managing Member, has served you with a drag-along notice concerning a proposed\ntransaction involving all six Company facilities. You hold 12% of Palisade Row Storage. The Agreement requires a\nresponse by the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt of the applicable notice. This advice uses only the 2017\nAgreement; I have not been given the notice, transaction documents, proof of service, approval records, or facts\nestablishing the deal’s legal form.\n\n1. Why I recommend dissent now\nSection 5.4 makes the execution duty conditional: “If a Sale of the Company has been approved under Section\n2.2,” each Member must consent, vote, and execute documents reasonably necessary to complete that Sale,\nincluding an interest assignment. A qualifying approval requires Florian and at least 75% of Percentage Interests\n(Section 2.2). The notice should identify whether the deal is asserted to be a Sale of the Company and provide\nmaterial documents then available (Section 4.2). We lack those facts, so I do not assume either compliance or\napproval.\nTransaction form is decisive. “Company” is Palisade Row Storage, LLC, not the facilities (Section 1.3). “Sale of the\nCompany” means a majority interest transfer or listed entity-level transaction (Section 1.4). A Major Transaction\nseparately includes an all-or-substantially-all asset sale (Section 1.5), and Section 5.3 says an asset sale is carried\nout by the Company and does not become an interest transfer merely because it covers the business assets. The\nAgreement is silent about whether an asset sale followed by dissolution is a “Sale of the Company” for Section\n5.4. That unresolved line favors preserving your dissent position, not accepting an irreversible interest-transfer\nobligation.\n\n2. Competing constructions\nThe enforcing reading has force: the six facilities comprise one business; a coordinated sale of all, followed by\nwinding up, has the practical end of a company sale. Section 2.2 authorizes an approved Major Transaction at the\n75% threshold, and Article 5 aims to make an approved exit workable. A court could infer that a member should\nnot defeat an approved exit simply because it uses assets rather than interests.\nThe limiting reading is stronger on the text. Section 5.4 repeatedly says “Sale of the Company”; Section 1.4\nspecifies entity-interest and entity-level events; Sections 1.5 and 5.3 separately identify asset dispositions.\nSection 3.2 confirms that implementation language alone does not surrender an interest. Those distinctions\nmatter: an asset sale leaves proceeds with the Company until Article 6, while a company sale transfers\nmembership interests. Treating every all-asset sale as a drag would diminish the separate Dissent Notice\nmechanism in Section 5.5. The clause is not unambiguous as to an asset sale followed by dissolution.\n\n3. Risks and the opposite result\nIf a court adopts the enforcing reading, Section 5.4 binds you to execute reasonably necessary drag documents\nafter a qualifying approved Sale; a timely Dissent Notice does not stop an approved Major Transaction (Section\n5.5), and the Managing Member may use the limited attorney-in-fact remedy to complete a qualifying sale. The\npractical risk of dissent is that the transaction closes without your signature while you face uncertainty and cost\nover whether the drag applied.\nIf a court adopts the limiting reading, the drag does not bind you in an asset sale followed by dissolution. The\nCompany may still complete an approved asset Major Transaction, but your Dissent Notice preserves the Section\n5.4 issue and Article 6 distribution rights. It supplies neither a veto nor an appraisal formula, purchase price, or\nguaranteed payment. The practical risk of signing is accepting an interest-transfer framework before the records\nestablish one, weakening your ability to keep the Agreement’s asset/company distinction. As a 12% minority\nmember, the targeted Section 5.5 procedure is more protective. Signing is not equally advisable because it\nrelinquishes that procedural position while the form is unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n                              Palisade Row Storage — confidential agreement-based material\n\f4. Action before the response date\nBefore the fourteenth (14th) day after receipt, deliver a written Dissent Notice under Section 5.5 by a permitted\nSection 4.1 method. Identify the notice, state that the all-facility transaction appears to be an asset transaction\nunless documents establish otherwise, and reserve your right to contest Section 5.4’s application and receive any\nArticle 6 distribution. Do not state approval of a membership-interest sale. Preserve the drag notice, delivery\nevidence, the 2017 Agreement, transaction-document versions, and communications.\nRequest written confirmation of (i) transaction form—interest transfer/entity-level transaction or asset sale; (ii)\nSection 2.2 written approval and percentage-interest calculation; (iii) whether the notice invokes Section 5.4 or\n5.5; and (iv) whether dissolution and winding up follow closing. These are missing facts that could change the\nadvice. A reservation is grounded in Sections 5.5 and 7.3; it does not contradict dissent or prevent an approved\ntransaction.\n\n5. Transition and post-closing position\nThis is a practice mid-change: the decision window precedes final form, closing, and any dissolution. Dissent is\nthe immediate procedural choice, not a prediction that the Company cannot sell facilities. If it later dissolves,\nSection 6.1 requires the applicable decision to wind up; Section 6.2 directs the Managing Member to settle\nliabilities; and Section 6.3 distributes what is available after liabilities and winding-up expenses in Percentage\nInterests. Your 12% therefore governs allocation of what is available if Article 6 applies, not a dollar amount, date,\nvaluation, or priority beyond those terms. If documents establish a qualifying Sale of the Company, reassessment\nis required before an inconsistent execution position.\n\n6. Disclosure and scope\nI drafted the 2017 Operating Agreement while advising the Company. That matters because I wrote language\nnow requiring construction. I have identified both readings candidly and base this advice on the recorded text and\nstructure, not unrecorded drafting intent. This is advice to you and may be shared with Rookwood Partners and\nHollowpine Holdings only as appropriate; it is not advice to them or the Company. It interprets the supplied\nAgreement only. Transaction-document negotiation, execution mechanics, closing administration, tax, and\nvaluation require the responsible advisers and information outside this record.\n\nDecision\nMy clear recommendation is: DISSENT. Deliver the Section 5.5 Dissent Notice before the fourteenth (14th)\nday after receipt, preserve the records, and request the listed confirmations. This protects your 12% member\nposition while the Agreement’s unresolved line between a Sale of the Company and an asset sale followed by\ndissolution is tested; it does not promise payment or prevent a properly approved transaction from proceeding.\nRagnhild Laurent\nCounsel to Viktor Kirchner\n\n\n\n\n                             Palisade Row Storage — confidential agreement-based material\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX exists at the exact required top-level filename and opens as a substantive document.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Directory listing and successful DOCX parse.","grader_hint":"Check exact path and that the file is not empty or corrupt."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PDF exists at the exact required top-level filename and opens as a substantive PDF.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Directory listing and successful PDF parse.","grader_hint":"Check exact path and readable page content."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each deliverable is no more than four pages when rendered or opened.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Count PDF pages and render the DOCX with an office-compatible renderer if needed."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter is addressed to Viktor Kirchner and identifies Ragnhild Laurent as counsel or adviser.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Letter heading, salutation, and signature block.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent professional identification."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies Palisade Row Storage and the proposed transaction involving all six facilities.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening factual section.","grader_hint":"Look for both the company and six-facility transaction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter accurately states that Viktor holds 12%.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ownership discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept 12 percent or equivalent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies Florian Oyelaran as the managing member who served the notice.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Factual background.","grader_hint":"Check role and notice attribution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter states the fourteen-day decision window without changing its duration.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Deadline discussion.","grader_hint":"Accept a qualified statement tied to the record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter distinguishes a transaction treated as a sale of the Company from an asset sale followed by dissolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Issue and analysis sections.","grader_hint":"The distinction must affect the legal analysis, not merely appear as two labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter quotes or accurately paraphrases the drag condition requiring a sale of the Company.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Drag-clause analysis.","grader_hint":"Verify against the supplied agreement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter applies the agreement’s defined meaning of Company or equivalent defined transaction language rather than analyzing the drag clause in isolation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions and application analysis.","grader_hint":"Compare the cited definition and the conclusion drawn from it with the source."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter recognizes that the agreement is silent or unclear about an asset sale followed by dissolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Ambiguity discussion.","grader_hint":"Do not award for calling the clause simply clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains the textual reading that supports enforcement of the drag in the proposed structure.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Supporting construction.","grader_hint":"Require agreement-based reasoning, not a bare assertion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains the strongest textual or structural reading that limits the drag to an actual sale of the Company.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opposing construction.","grader_hint":"Look for the best counterargument rather than a straw man."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The competing interpretations are weighed against the agreement’s structure and purpose with a reasoned tradeoff.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Synthesis of interpretive analysis.","grader_hint":"A conclusion without comparative reasoning does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter gives exactly one primary recommendation: sign the drag or dissent.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive recommendation and conclusion.","grader_hint":"Do not treat an unranked list as a recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The recommendation is clearly linked to the ambiguity and the client’s 12% position.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale.","grader_hint":"The advice must be client-specific."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter says plainly what Viktor’s position becomes if a court adopts the opposite construction.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Counterfactual consequences section.","grader_hint":"Require a concrete statement about whether the drag binds him or does not, and the resulting position."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter connects the recommended choice to the agreement’s treatment of member consent, transfer, or transaction implementation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational legal analysis.","grader_hint":"Check the source provision and application."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter addresses the effect of dissent on the proposed transaction rather than treating dissent as a generic valuation election.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dissent analysis.","grader_hint":"Award only where the effect is tied to the record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains what Viktor must do before the fourteen-day window closes.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action paragraph.","grader_hint":"The action must be consistent with the single recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter distinguishes the deadline for making the decision from later closing or dissolution events.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing analysis.","grader_hint":"Require both the immediate window and later transaction state."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies the transaction as a practice mid-change and explains its relevance during the transition.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition-period discussion.","grader_hint":"Generic mention of change without consequences is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains the recommended position’s implication after closing or dissolution, not only during the notice period.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state analysis.","grader_hint":"Check continuity from present decision to post-transaction status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter states the relevant agreement provisions governing dissolution or winding up.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dissolution section.","grader_hint":"Verify accuracy against the supplied agreement."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains how dissolution and distribution provisions bear on Viktor’s position without converting them into a guaranteed payout.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dissolution and economic consequence analysis.","grader_hint":"Look for conditional, agreement-grounded treatment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter discloses that Ragnhild Laurent drafted the 2017 operating agreement.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conflict or disclosure paragraph.","grader_hint":"The disclosure must be explicit, not implied."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The drafting-role disclosure is handled candidly and explains why it matters to the present interpretation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Disclosure and independence language.","grader_hint":"A bare biographical statement is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter separates interpretation of the agreement from advice on transaction execution.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope statement and analysis.","grader_hint":"Check that legal interpretation remains the letter’s core work."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter marks tax advice as outside the opinion or identifies the need for tax advice without giving unsupported tax conclusions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope boundary.","grader_hint":"Either clear limitation is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter marks valuation advice as outside the opinion or identifies the need for valuation advice without inventing value.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope boundary.","grader_hint":"No unsupported valuation conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter marks transaction administration or closing mechanics as belonging to another function where applicable.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope boundary.","grader_hint":"Accept a precise limitation tied to the record."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies assumptions or missing facts that could change the advice.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions and limitations section.","grader_hint":"Examples may include transaction form, notice contents, or proof of service, but must be relevant and not invented as facts."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter distinguishes facts taken from the agreement from inferences and unresolved factual questions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Throughout the letter.","grader_hint":"Assess disciplined use of factual qualifiers."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies the agreement as the 2017 operating agreement.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Source description.","grader_hint":"Check against the supplied file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter accurately describes the drag notice’s relationship to the agreement’s drag-along mechanism.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Notice analysis.","grader_hint":"Require more than repeating that a notice was served."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter evaluates whether the proposed all-facility transaction fits the agreement’s transaction vocabulary.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Application section.","grader_hint":"Compare transaction facts with the source definitions and operative clauses."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter addresses the possibility that the facilities are sold as assets and the entity is then dissolved.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transaction-form analysis.","grader_hint":"The sequence must be explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter identifies the strongest practical risk of signing.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk discussion.","grader_hint":"The risk must follow from the agreement and recommendation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter identifies the strongest practical risk of dissenting.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk discussion.","grader_hint":"Require a distinct risk, not a restatement of the signing risk."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter explains why the apparent alternative to the recommendation is not equally advisable on the present record.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation synthesis.","grader_hint":"This is the normative judgment separating advice from a menu."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter recommends preserving the drag notice and relevant agreement materials.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action or records paragraph.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent preservation advice."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter identifies at least one confirmation or document Viktor should obtain before acting, tied to an uncertainty in the record.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Action paragraph.","grader_hint":"The request must be specific and relevant, not a generic request for more information."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any reservation of rights is stated only to the extent supported by the agreement and does not contradict the recommendation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reservation language.","grader_hint":"Award for a grounded reservation; do not require a reservation if the agreement does not support one."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter names the relevant drag-along provision or defined term sufficiently for Viktor to locate it in the agreement.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Citation or pinpoint reference.","grader_hint":"Accept section numbers or an accurate descriptive locator."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter uses the agreement’s member-consent or approval threshold accurately where it bears on the drag.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Consent analysis.","grader_hint":"Verify threshold and conditions against the source; do not award for an unsupported threshold."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter accurately applies any agreement requirement concerning notice, service, or election mechanics.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Procedure and timing analysis.","grader_hint":"Check every asserted mechanic against the source."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter accurately applies any agreement provision governing distributions or priority after a sale or dissolution.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Economic consequences section.","grader_hint":"Do not reward invented priorities."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter does not promise that dissenters’ rights guarantee a particular payment or valuation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire letter.","grader_hint":"Treat careful qualification as satisfying the item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The letter does not introduce invented case names, statutes, transaction amounts, dates, or counterparties.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entire letter compared with the supplied record.","grader_hint":"Minor harmless drafting conventions do not count; unsupported substantive facts do."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The language is specific and unambiguous about what Viktor should do, by when, and why.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation and action sections.","grader_hint":"Assess usability by a client under deadline."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter is readable by a client without sacrificing the distinctions necessary for legal analysis.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document.","grader_hint":"Look for defined terms, short paragraphs, meaningful numbering, and avoidance of unexplained jargon."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter ends with the decision or a direct reiteration of the recommendation, not with an unranked next-steps list.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final paragraph.","grader_hint":"A short action reminder before the final decision is acceptable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX and PDF contain the same primary recommendation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Side-by-side text comparison.","grader_hint":"Normalize whitespace and compare the substantive conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The DOCX and PDF agree on Viktor’s 12% interest, the fourteen-day period, and every other shared figure or date.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Extracted text comparison.","grader_hint":"Any discrepancy in a shared factual value fails this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PDF faithfully renders the DOCX’s headings, numbered decision points, caveats, and conclusion without omitted or unreadable substantive content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":true,"required":false,"evidence":"Visual comparison of rendered DOCX and PDF.","grader_hint":"Check for clipping, missing pages, broken tables, or lost text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The letter presents a defensible normative position rather than merely describing ambiguity.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall legal advice.","grader_hint":"The writer must commit after acknowledging uncertainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The completed package is suitable for downstream peer review or approval, with source-based reasoning traceable from facts to recommendation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall artifact quality and citations.","grader_hint":"Assess professional completeness, traceability, and absence of unsupported assertions."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a minority-member advice package prepared during a live transaction and a change in the company’s operating state. The reviewer needs a defensible construction of an ambiguous drag clause, a clear client decision, and careful limits around transaction, tax, valuation, and administration issues handled by other functions.","difficulty_sources":["Interpreting an ambiguous drag-along condition against defined terms and dissolution provisions","Committing to one client recommendation while fairly presenting the strongest opposing construction","Transforming one operating agreement into a concise advice letter and matching PDF","Managing transition-period and post-dissolution consequences","Disclosing prior drafting involvement and marking boundaries with tax, valuation, and transaction administration functions"],"authoring_plan":{"source_construction":{"filename":"palisade_row_storage_2017_operating_agreement.pdf","purpose":"Create one internally consistent synthetic operating agreement for Palisade Row Storage. It should include the company name, six-facility business description, Viktor Kirchner’s 12% membership interest, Florian Oyelaran’s managing-member role, the 2017 date, and the relevant defined terms.","required_substance":["Include a drag-along clause conditioned on a sale of the Company, with a controlling-member approval mechanism and ordinary execution obligations.","Define Company and Sale of the Company in language that supports a plausible argument that a sale of substantially all business assets may or may not qualify, but do not resolve the asset-sale-followed-by-dissolution question expressly.","Include provisions on member approval, notice, the fourteen-day election or response period, dissenters’ rights or an equivalent contractual election, and the effect of failing to respond.","Include transaction implementation language that makes the drag’s consequences relevant to a member signing or refusing.","Include dissolution, winding-up, and distribution provisions sufficient to analyze Viktor’s position after an asset sale followed by dissolution.","Include conflict, amendment, and dispute or construction provisions only where they help a lawyer reason from the agreement; avoid adding a controlling clause that makes the central ambiguity disappear.","Use only synthetic identifiers and no real addresses, account numbers, or external authorities."],"imperfections":["The agreement must be complete and mutually consistent but deliberately silent or structurally ambiguous about whether an asset sale followed by dissolution is a sale of the Company for drag purposes.","The notice language and transaction provisions should create a genuine interpretive tension without containing a hidden answer.","The agreement should not supply a transaction price or other invented economic facts."]},"gold_derivation":{"method":"Have a domain-expert lawyer draft a four-page-or-shorter client advice letter from the agreement, then independently review it against every rubric item and render both formats.","intended_verdict":"The gold must choose exactly one of sign or dissent, explain both plausible constructions, disclose the drafting role, describe the opposite-reading consequence for Viktor, and give a practical action inside fourteen days. The exact recommendation may be selected by the expert based on the constructed agreement’s balance, but the reasoning must be coherent and agreement-grounded.","cross_file_rule":"Generate the PDF from the reviewed DOCX or compare both after rendering; all names, percentages, dates, defined terms, caveats, and the recommendation must match.","quality_review":["Verify that no rubric criterion depends on an unstated transaction amount, private code, or external live source.","Verify that the letter does not guarantee a dissenters’ payment, invent authorities, or overstep into tax, valuation, or administrative advice.","Verify the transition-period and post-dissolution consequences are both addressed.","Verify the final paragraph is a decision, not an unranked next-steps list."]}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"primary_plus_appendix","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"ref_palisade_2017_oa","filename":"palisade_row_storage_2017_operating_agreement.pdf","purpose":"The sole company record for construing the drag-along provision, ownership, definitions, transaction mechanics, notice timing, dissolution, distributions, dispute provisions, and related member rights.","required":true,"discovery":"Read this PDF before drafting; do not assume facts not stated in it."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"advice_docx","filename":"viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A completed client advice letter giving Viktor one actionable recommendation on signing the drag-along or asserting dissenters’ rights.","required":true,"constraints":["Top level of the starting working directory; no more than four pages including tables or callouts; legally precise, client-readable, and internally consistent with the PDF."],"rendering_required":true},{"deliverable_id":"advice_pdf","filename":"viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A rendered, matching PDF version of the client advice letter.","required":true,"constraints":["Top level of the starting working directory; content must match the DOCX, including dates, percentages, amounts, defined terms, recommendation, and caveats; legible and no more than four pages."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"legal_policy_document","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"lawyers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"One domain-expert legal reviewer checks source fidelity, interpretive reasoning, professional disclosure, scope boundaries, client usability, and cross-deliverable agreement; a second independent reviewer checks rendering and rubric reachability.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The source is synthetic and intentionally omits jurisdiction-specific law and transaction economics, so the gold must remain conditional where the agreement does not answer a question. The rubric evaluates defensible agreement-based advice rather than one immutable legal conclusion."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"结构/材料验收记录","detail":"validated 属于结构、防泄漏、材料推进验收；没有内容正确率结论。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T01:23:12+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T01:23:12+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T01:23:12+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T01:23:12+00:00"},{"check":"instruction_rubric_overlap","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":false,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-25T01:23:12+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_c3/runs/c1eeb8fb3c3a97a5","declared_deliverables":["viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.docx","viktor_kirchner_drag_along_advice.pdf"]};
